#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124109 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "da9684b4-df8d-531d-a200-cf54685067c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10913" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/cf01827dc23a26ed9d8d81333d735021d3a4eb494a434ba9fc38f1d110dfcc25.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <        \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <    \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/0af9e8b587bbeb640ff9ec0cdc8197a9d99e8f5282772561b92ba74915cf1226.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point because there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124110 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "7e78e3a1-836a-53af-95a3-0a9d192cc7d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-10924" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/38057eabc400c888e287be8dbbd8b4de172b625fa5922188f99f71112213ef3b.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-\\infty, 2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $x_0\\in\\mathbb R$ is the set of points whose distance from $x_0$ is *smaller than* $\\delta{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\{ x : |  x- x_0| <           \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of $x_0$ is given by the open interval $( x_0-\\delta,  x_0+\\delta),$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/3e3aa5b8e0e93f7fda306a6f39c999af9a587614795316ce12fff8da2c9c2dd2.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/389aa307a3c0c10b5d51b86bed6e05331fb9299379652f06e65a4a7b9f776ead.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists any neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained *outside* the set $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/1c0992788a0ac7ce8cab2caabb41479ba2267e532412d1ef2c7115f56afb911c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124117 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "1f21955b-70dc-5b61-8b19-42860de1be4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-11031" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y > 4-2x\\big\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2: y = 4-2x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1, 2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\n- The point $P(1, 2)$ belongs to $A$\n\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <                   \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <               \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie above the line $y=4-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/8e8e3296c59277b92eddddd3deacd7149fd9d7062aa05f4858e1846c3fb3238b.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/077daf974625dd03a02b3f1c55e7b60c5a8c9686bc5515d78246bf7e1048e4cf.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1, 2)$ satisfy the equation $y = 4-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\\partial A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1, 2)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y > 4-2x.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124118 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/id #uuid "9b01032c-d4e6-5660-a3c9-477e67c011a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11032" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $\\partial A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA=\\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1 \\bigg\\}, \\qquad \\partial A = \\bigg\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:  \\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} =1 \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n\n- The point $P(1, 1)$ belongs to $A$\n\n- The point $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point $\\mathbf x_0$ is the set of points whose distance from $\\mathbf x_0$ is less than $\\delta.$ \n\n$$\n\\{\\mathbf x : |\\mathbf x-\\mathbf x_0| <                  \\delta \\}\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ is the open disc $|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf x_0| <              \\delta,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point $\\mathbf x_0$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} = 1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/d70dec41d9d5d24d82d1efafd65f1898ffcd5794dca98ca46d486cd3be428a18.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1, 1)$ and $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/3e99fed83e9e31f223bfb4c1a9df87aa0b54e7865c1bfbc5b175b684c7c3c58a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $Q\\left(-\\sqrt 2,2\\right)$ satisfy the equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} = 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $\\partial A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1, 1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + \\dfrac{y^2}{8} \\geq 1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $A$ and points that do not belong to $A.$ Therefore, the point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29c50646-8bb4-5c86-b8c9-fb60e185fce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311623} {:answer/id #uuid "8f71774a-7fd4-5d0a-92ea-cfa7919f83a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311624} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6759d8-6310-54b8-bb32-885b804d4718" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311625} {:answer/id #uuid "4e309dac-77d4-5aa1-a0f8-25f0bc204c52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311626} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8c9df2-9fb1-50e4-813a-aebb5c4dc155" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f71774a-7fd4-5d0a-92ea-cfa7919f83a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311624} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcdbdb9b-9400-5381-88a3-6c5622c5fd39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311622}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46f5a0b4-83ed-5543-bc0b-e0e8b5ba9737" :question/math-academy-id "q-105313" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/da3040b284606927a6653bfe18697bff0032dea2647d8d36d857c97472bbc540.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/c1491f04d1e3d4692dc362008b13ff3909e7ae6f554a5032a9a93036ee2452b5.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $B$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6b69b8c-4d69-5245-9e36-73fe46dc7036" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311630} {:answer/id #uuid "9f3489d5-b3b7-51f7-aa54-ac85a894edca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311631} {:answer/id #uuid "4ccb7856-2160-50ef-a220-d9e2aac578e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311632} {:answer/id #uuid "5c485f1e-9c81-5689-8256-7f86fb54e7be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311633} {:answer/id #uuid "aec28d92-de68-5328-9fb2-6d6a08c8ec59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311634}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f3489d5-b3b7-51f7-aa54-ac85a894edca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311631} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d0569d8-0022-55db-ac49-15117caebba7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8cb1acdc-135d-5511-a5f1-370cbcf1e0b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-105334" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ is a boundary point of $A$\n- The point $Q(2,2)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/a1371f9abdeae5083171f5ddfa5d3e237b7e223ba2ba5cf8a822322c34a4676c.png)\n\n \n\nLet's add the points $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ and $Q(2,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/021ebd260ca399ee47bf8268d02a1a05019d63eef8ab2f5facc56f8576195a80.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ do not satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>4.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is fully contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3999ef0-11e0-5b59-9290-1b872f780d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311637} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ac752b-221b-541a-a232-08060fc79940" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311638} {:answer/id #uuid "55113adc-1021-5829-9a0d-b6e8ef4f6775" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311639} {:answer/id #uuid "db29932e-a16b-5b1d-8f61-bca02194ee07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311640} {:answer/id #uuid "dc5941f3-f421-5ba6-9a32-3d58823aeeda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc5941f3-f421-5ba6-9a32-3d58823aeeda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311641} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac5d8e0f-07eb-5f3c-aea8-b01f44dcea6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d495c61-1ca7-53d4-97c5-b1dfb0532c83" :question/math-academy-id "q-105357" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 9},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(3,0)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(3,0)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(0,2)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or outside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/b9b92085d112ab87592aa8c4d6a56aa4a61ea859d560bac316ce8c97a0af20e5.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,0)$ and $Q(0,2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/27806c9bb67b60d52cfbae8e881d307d71a3a5fb9465f36d978035f74d78f4bc.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(3,0)$ satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(3,0)$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311642 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "660fecd5-fcdc-5b17-83ed-176fc9fb9629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311644} {:answer/id #uuid "669185a7-adc2-5f53-ac01-5a3a7b0b6c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311645} {:answer/id #uuid "a622f23e-abd0-5228-a32d-5b5adb140ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311646} {:answer/id #uuid "0566ee1c-d1e3-5f1c-aa20-de67202ad825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311647} {:answer/id #uuid "2451e525-4b9b-576e-92d7-df60128f86ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2451e525-4b9b-576e-92d7-df60128f86ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311648} :answer-field/id #uuid "727aeb45-c7d6-5a45-97e8-4979f05cd300" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae714c8f-15bc-5435-b67e-3f69ed7208e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105365" :question/problem "The set $B$ and its boundary $∂B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12},\\,∂B={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(3,4)$ lies on the boundary of $B$\n- The point $P(3,4)$ belongs to $B$\n- The point $Q(-3,6)$ is an interior point of $B$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $B$ consists of all points that lie inside the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/adee860d39b4d9c4511db2b2f1ad6e7f46342cc474da47303af742be1cc911b3.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(3,4)$ and $Q(-3,6)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/2185e7129cd799eb1cbb888789566a406b1594dd5a649b9129bec491a1dce1c7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ does not satisfy the equation $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=12.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie on the boundary of $B.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(3,4)$ satisfy the inequality $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<12.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $B.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Every open neighborhood of $Q$ contains points that belong to $B$ and points that do not belong to $B.$ The point $Q$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffe6a10-945f-5055-8704-77610ca7af6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121298} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b739bc-83a2-509c-8257-8d25508a0af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121299} {:answer/id #uuid "e15e9680-a271-5911-bb6c-338b25f55e7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121300} {:answer/id #uuid "e243f6e6-5585-57ec-8a09-91149180f286" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121301} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc271ad-d07a-5dd8-87ac-0db115d40bae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9b739bc-83a2-509c-8257-8d25508a0af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121299} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c6d1461-4ff5-5329-ad4d-8ef22820d89e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98524e7d-f97e-5125-b634-0488b239406e" :question/math-academy-id "q-156152" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/9685260f754e0d6333de6f24ffbf3908c055d6e4fe2d3ffe47391410735c7df2.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[-2,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $A$ is not an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $A$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/39f7097f82f19d703208f9e4e8638e4d64aa417ad9cf4bc1f90092eb0c5e5639.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $B$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/3ffa79d3052247aecd276c7794a498340acb66a1e74aeddcdd7800b1e9704506.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/f116e950d99e0a00908c6c40a3c788cc450efa120843369e13f857cb084d8a1e.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6db01141-a056-551b-86c8-13bda2c9e243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311651} {:answer/id #uuid "0a2c00d0-2272-59b3-abb3-36bf80462d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311652} {:answer/id #uuid "a16d0846-d9d5-505f-96b2-8a148f60f982" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311653} {:answer/id #uuid "32b4d1e5-26fc-5759-a94f-7624f6c7ee2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311654} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e80046-3810-5ed9-a543-14eda7f0cc4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db01141-a056-551b-86c8-13bda2c9e243" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311651} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a147d81-eddc-5277-b9a6-09732c3ea4b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "136272e9-db22-5ecb-9045-90af32b1d84d" :question/math-academy-id "q-158536" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le 2},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(2,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-2,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=2.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/de056be6665a342dc8569e0db330743a96e08fa770798c94ec7dd0cc4e987d92.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,2)$ and $Q(-2,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/c6c1237dfa5f8fed76bad01466c94442d27825c4edab7dbc92a99b177af4a0b6.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the equation $y=2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(2,2)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le 2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $Q$ is an interior point of $A$ since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained in $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a9b74be-a849-50c3-b517-5d1e2d75b647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311658} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fe8ca7-ad15-577c-b93d-305c8040c957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311659} {:answer/id #uuid "deffb945-f7b0-5a27-92c5-1c0c75fb1d4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311660} {:answer/id #uuid "c46ff3ba-29bc-559d-8020-799f42a057d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311661} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6b377d-2c59-5aee-bf38-450dce2ebb70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a9b74be-a849-50c3-b517-5d1e2d75b647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311658} :answer-field/id #uuid "fab9ed48-7eae-570c-9a5e-2c618484b886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311657}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b108c228-d4d0-508a-b1b2-0caa496efcd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-158538" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x\\le 1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x=1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $Q(2,-2)$ is an interior point of $A$\n- The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or to the left of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/119fbce04df572e5b1c083432d0183da1cc04add5e4ad56ac7704af3cf8ee024.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(2,-2)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/7dc96640505a272ec9701a9d155f7ed2809e6f6df29ac5327af6b2b2324fa162.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the equation $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality $x\\le 1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bf33699-2bf0-5309-b383-42c8cdc868d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311665} {:answer/id #uuid "f14484ee-0675-5dbe-a3e3-7bcf1848f287" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311666} {:answer/id #uuid "ef14238f-d7cc-56c3-aa13-8a1613f218c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311667} {:answer/id #uuid "c281c0a0-7b30-5679-9277-61de2fa6ff5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311668} {:answer/id #uuid "333b1421-bbad-5976-8440-daaaf3e4d14c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef14238f-d7cc-56c3-aa13-8a1613f218c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311667} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8fba19c-7d29-53df-998a-3e791a4972e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311664}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89770747-57a6-5885-af6d-40679fee1c91" :question/math-academy-id "q-158541" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\ge 3x-1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x-1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(0,-3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(0,-3)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or above the line $y=3x-1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/7df3b0c7e884325519ea15f5f949d4b254ef4f63f2449fd36a7aa811ff1b385f.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(0,-3)$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/7f710e625802403ca5a34f4331cb2998272479c72deca0e24a6116072e4afb5f.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ do not satisfy the equation $y=3x-1.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(0,-3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y\\ge 3x-1.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $Q$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $Q$ that is entirely contained inside the set $A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311670 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c9a4b23-d6e6-54e9-b36f-8af113e0e877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311672} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1fa554-88ed-5fcd-a8e0-507d846ee1f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311673} {:answer/id #uuid "59b5c141-b2cb-536a-b9a6-d2de0a8d94de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311674} {:answer/id #uuid "e73ded0c-90ca-5649-b7f8-06a72724e0a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311675} {:answer/id #uuid "181fd387-3f81-54f2-ab3f-b0be739d7dd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59b5c141-b2cb-536a-b9a6-d2de0a8d94de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311674} :answer-field/id #uuid "1738ac35-567a-50a3-8fbb-8a4bfa5643da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311671}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1c4f1c5-3d71-5d37-b712-2a2d8cc53891" :question/math-academy-id "q-158562" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<1-2x},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=1-2x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(-1,3)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(-1,3)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(1,-1)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=1-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/7ce1ca6af3caf2b454d7fed484f98d11478839bcf107c0f19562688c7cdaca6a.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-1,3)$ and $Q(1,-1)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/aad8bc7bfd64af0dd1e08618626ae3237bb4aba42be1f91fe7ff9328ad6ecbe7.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ satisfy $y=1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(-1,3)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<1-2x.$ Hence, $P$ is not in $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $Q$ is *not* an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7d6b539-595f-5483-b8ac-6f8a57496e84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311679} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbd2c95-ecb3-58e5-9d97-87affe644840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311680} {:answer/id #uuid "99cfcd05-7285-592a-a4d3-f7e3b3a237b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311681} {:answer/id #uuid "bdea72de-5e5d-5ca5-b4ee-43f050281a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311682} {:answer/id #uuid "96cd8b22-2d24-58c9-a540-83225d1761fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99cfcd05-7285-592a-a4d3-f7e3b3a237b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311681} :answer-field/id #uuid "b07b2009-bf4d-55a5-bb7c-cda6b6d5ff21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b3841d0e-b588-5614-a146-d64770e033f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-158603" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x>1},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x=1}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,1)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(0,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie to the right of the line $x=1.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/7dc932c43ed153f4ac1832ca136f9035d879ea1f54e01c69e7a032b7a53d4940.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,1)$ and $Q(0,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/06fdba4e26ebad28ef3f63106b7bdd8cb115b91a5f0076370a27b12f05added3.png)\n\n \n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ satisfy $x=1.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The coordinates of $P(1,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $x>1.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f4c8266-9d5e-513d-ac12-dde3f4096ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311686} {:answer/id #uuid "2b6b1535-d7d7-50ec-ad20-d016f6d52a39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311687} {:answer/id #uuid "95a643e5-cfb0-5160-8064-5a0e2955ce06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311688} {:answer/id #uuid "216d16d5-fd6c-5326-b390-c3ae20eadebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311689} {:answer/id #uuid "74afdf56-e880-5d20-abda-3e64c083a92e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f4c8266-9d5e-513d-ac12-dde3f4096ad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311686} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6b976c2-c1d8-523d-90b5-e9c01be07de7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311685}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ed4af63-2e6e-55b3-8327-f9c36e3bc93b" :question/math-academy-id "q-158684" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/5dccdb1f8957bb15d7b2215320c6e632819a647bf4dc350ff850876bae6298ec.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-3,2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/d595e29d31743694c404b99c79c60e40b152d30083e633d4269ad4444be3f826.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/2336ff8a65dd397e8324384b9f9536f3c7d4ad2d76a81caeba28d7fe3ec229ef.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/26903667d45cecf81539025c4cda333129cf89600821b383f20d574c75e537b0.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311691 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81c4abce-09ab-5d69-8bbf-2764df1037b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311693} {:answer/id #uuid "e06c8c50-e024-5998-8467-09f3f6f7a9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311694} {:answer/id #uuid "fe0f907d-2554-55a9-9f91-88c50de6ebe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311695} {:answer/id #uuid "167c3bd9-8962-56ca-b477-aa5e58c7f92d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311696} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4d7657-87d8-52e6-b749-aea97c49983d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe0f907d-2554-55a9-9f91-88c50de6ebe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311695} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bef1826-f048-59d5-a40a-8beffa82699c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311692}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "202c3f4d-e7d4-5eab-99a6-411be823de39" :question/math-academy-id "q-158685" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/73f61de1a716545449439ed8875b465dc5aec6f3148e520ad028a96725f2a4f0.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[0,1]\\cup {2}$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained in the set $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/84afb5331ef9244120d9f3d9b14e25fa2d19da31f8eb685c5d74b089e0d99289.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/c0b35fafc6e628c36e41fa8df42e3a477d8aac4af0b4c3819fa0063e5bd014df.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ Notice that $C$ is a point of $M$ and $C$ belongs to any open neighborhood of $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/e54fa85543a79c18e00d3b03281d6774c98fdb03e9b5440b8f179ce5a0d09519.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and III only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311698 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "093cdbe2-e256-5ef2-ad6c-4c9778253b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311700} {:answer/id #uuid "c6c97ea9-1d29-5de8-948d-7ea6bd2f7151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311701} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8e0076-8b60-52e3-a002-cda96ad77278" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311702} {:answer/id #uuid "d97ea544-af45-51c9-ba7e-423967db02ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311703} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa60823-c070-5a49-be42-91a1e339c877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fa60823-c070-5a49-be42-91a1e339c877" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311704} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e5f302-1e1d-5df2-b21f-54f9c80a3aff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ad095d07-7882-57cc-832c-a566567a104d" :question/math-academy-id "q-158731" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{y}^{2}\\ge -2x},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{y}^{2}=-2x}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(-2,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(-2,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(-1,0)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nThe set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or to the right of the parabola ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/e5547cb8e0ed0fec1fd2e515a1bcd9009044289dfdc516c6a6cba14b650a7645.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-2,2)$ and $Q(-1,0)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/7b353dd43fd293424ba8404184e53869d41d8a1b73493d845bc85aa333130606.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the equation ${y}^{2}=-2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies on the boundary of the set.\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${y}^{2}\\ge -2x.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I and II only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24b9af85-3717-59e0-aa72-d701f3c6b6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Nonlinear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf8f1840-5d55-5dea-8d2c-b02f21aabcd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311707} {:answer/id #uuid "0d1bf680-3c9f-535c-a429-8e6cf0ebab21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311708} {:answer/id #uuid "9d655f20-9a39-5b3c-9fa8-37d4aef88322" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311709} {:answer/id #uuid "dfd1f73c-96d6-51d0-a8b1-0926f55d7805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311710} {:answer/id #uuid "b1cd480d-216d-5bdd-8fb5-173652b7c5f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1cd480d-216d-5bdd-8fb5-173652b7c5f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311711} :answer-field/id #uuid "f264de83-f994-5cad-8e79-1df364630719" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d259a9e0-0f9d-5a71-aa41-b6579973f567" :question/math-academy-id "q-158733" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The point $P(1,2)$ lies on the boundary of $A$\n- The point $P(1,2)$ belongs to $A$\n- The point $Q(3,3)$ is an interior point of $A$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie inside the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/181a21e9975320e54365ef10bd801a2fa6494058dd40cda79cb29b713bd8df84.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(1,2)$ and $Q(3,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/15d42de861a3e687a269326e8338ea5d99b6a5a609f4a20e597e64ccc4a8474a.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ do not satisfy the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The coordinates of $P(1,2)$ satisfy the inequality ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a2db578-561a-537c-a425-edbd04491a83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311714} {:answer/id #uuid "15918ad7-0468-5496-92e1-1a8a339f30c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311715} {:answer/id #uuid "06ee1ae5-d79e-5ba6-be98-0f1e2eb97817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311716} {:answer/id #uuid "2dadaa7f-e37e-5a89-9e98-ea16d4b2c92a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311717} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbb397f-691a-5632-b60c-361b4b86889f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dadaa7f-e37e-5a89-9e98-ea16d4b2c92a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311717} :answer-field/id #uuid "199add5c-e0a7-58dd-82e7-f432a6b09f83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4817945-28a4-57ce-8cfb-2e238aba21e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159264" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/07463b409d54d1970ee9d47cad5de7188ea44e682a58e7e941b3cd4bf7f5efa6.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=(-1,1)$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The point $A$ is *not* a boundary point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/734ff08a25e25486dc65f919ce99bc5422ca8ce264bb7d044479b1eb3b6a716b.png)\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $B$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/286f8b397b3c908d29678640d691558a784e31751d09c68e6b6da855c272231c.png)\n\n- Statement III is true. The point $C$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $C$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041362/assets/7ed7726fa1a139ded4a9226bcb01c17d0ad46ef4bd03a820f033640c68778453.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'III only.'"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7567e89b-29b5-5f7b-b120-008e735f9a81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311721} {:answer/id #uuid "31f9b996-fd45-5cef-92f8-8bc4220fe688" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311722} {:answer/id #uuid "9daef2fb-2790-516a-8c43-168d2813a6fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218